# knee-rn9py -- the control-recipe leave-one-out at 224px. Every one of these is the CHAMPION FAMILY'S
# OWN RECIPE with one fragment removed, so each is by construction weaker than probe224_control_s2026
# (0.8914 @ledger75, 909 rows). They are declined as SEATS and kept as EVIDENCE: together they are the
# honest account of what separates this recipe from the .903 reference. Re-open any one of them only by
# answering why a deliberately-crippled control belongs in a pool its own parent misses.

ctl_nodrop_s2026
  Control minus slot_dropout (0.40 -> 0.0): 0.8909 @ledger75, -0.0005. The knob the drop40_long family is
  NAMED for, and it is flat. Declined as a seat; the null is the finding.

ctl_maxpool_s2026
  Control with labelwise pooling swapped for max: 0.8851 @ledger75, -0.0063. Labelwise pays about 1.5 board
  ticks over max. Declined as a seat.

ctl_nocalib_s2026
  Control minus calibrated_targets: 0.8814 @ledger75, -0.0100. The largest single-knob payer in the sweep.
  Declined as a seat.

ctl_norank_s2026
  Control minus the ranked objective (rank_weight 1.0 -> 0.0): 0.8860 @ledger75, -0.0054. Declined as a seat.

ctl_nofloor_s2026
  Control minus weight_floor, so an unmentioned finding stops pulling at 0.25: 0.8883 @ledger75, -0.0031.
  Declined as a seat.

ctl_nowhole_s2026
  Control minus whole_stack: 0.8523 @ledger75, -0.0391 -- eight times the next largest fragment and past the
  1.5% bar on its own. whole_stack is the recipe's spine, not a knob. CAVEAT carried with the number:
  convergence_check reads UNDERTRAINED (best e11 of 12) and the arm ran at 13s/epoch against the control's
  ~60s, so the recipe is tuned for a stack this arm does not have -- 0.8523 is a FLOOR for the variant.
  Declined as a seat; kept as the sweep's one gate-clearing reading.

ctl_fastbb_s2026
  Control with backbone_lr 4e-5 -> 1e-4: 0.8699 @ledger75, -0.0215, the second-largest fragment and past the
  1.5% bar. Unlike the other six this one moves a CONTINUOUS knob rather than removing a piece, so it carries
  a derivative sign: the control sits on the fast side of its own optimum. convergence_check UNDERTRAINED
  (best e11 of 12). Declined as a seat; kept as the sweep's one actionable direction, followed up in knee-lm2ol.

ctl_stripall_s2026
  The knee-jmct4 composition test: the control with all five small knobs stripped at once, 0.8645 @ledger75,
  -0.0269 against 0.8914. Existed to test whether the five separate arms' +0.0253 composes, and it does
  (predicted 0.8661, landed 0.0016 under, inside the pre-registered band). Converged, e9 of 12.
  Declined as a seat; kept as the measurement that turned a summed number into a measured one.

ctl_slowbb_s2026
  Control with backbone_lr 4e-5 -> 2e-5: 0.8904 @ledger75, -0.0010, converged at e10 -- the same peak epoch
  as the control, so this null is not budget-bound. With ctl_fastbb's -0.0215 at 2.5x it puts 4e-5 at the
  optimum and shows the optimum is asymmetric: a plateau on the slow side, a cliff on the fast side.
  Declined as a seat; it closes the last open fragment of the control recipe (knee-lm2ol).

band_dense_s2026_n24b0.1-0.9
  A READING, not a member. `band_dense_s2026_n24b0.1-0.9.holdout.npz` (2026-09-11) is the line-65 seat's
  own weights re-read through a 24-slice / 0.1-0.9 decode; there is no checkpoint of that name and there
  never was one. Priced on the same 909 rows it reads 0.8598 midpoint / 0.8888 ledger75 at epoch 8,
  against band_dense_s2026's 0.8578 / 0.8877 -- a real +0.0011 at the 75 cut, directional and below the
  1.5% bar. It sat on the roster as a seat until 2026-09-16, where it matched no file and therefore made
  the whole roster unable to build a package (knee-aqppc). Declined on the mechanism rather than the
  number: package_members ships `.pt` files, so nothing added to the shelf can reach this. Re-open it by
  changing the KERNEL's decode over the line-65 seat, or by training the spec into its own checkpoint.

labelwise_depth24_drop40_long_ff
  The whole-corpus `--fold -1 --ema` refit of the depth24 drop40 labelwise seat. Shipped in `fullseat2_v1`
  as the second dose and read 0.946, identical to the champion carrying neither refit. See the next line:
  the dose series over 0/1/2 refit seats is 0.946/0.946/0.946 and the argument is the same for both.

labelwise_ext_drop40_long_ff
  The whole-corpus `--fold -1 --ema` refits of two of the champion's three drop40 labelwise seats. Both
  SHIPPED — `fullseat_v1` (ext only) and `fullseat2_v1` (both) — and both read 0.946, identical to
  `tripleseat_v2` with neither. The dose series over 0/1/2 refit seats is 0.946/0.946/0.946, so one extra
  fifth of the corpus at a 2/3 share over seven seats sits under the board's own 0.001 resolution.
  Declined as SEATS, not as checkpoints: they are mounted and rebuildable, and a future entry may seat
  them for a reason other than this one. Re-open it by naming a mechanism that is not "more data per
  seat" — that one is measured flat at two doses.
  RE-OPENED 2026-09-17 on those terms, as a SOLO and not as a seat (knee-22nk5, `kneemri-extffsolo`). The
  dose series is silent about the solo end by construction: a refit recovers information seven neighbours
  already supply between them, so flat at a 1-of-8 share is what a REAL solo term predicts. The solo term
  itself is n=1 (`l75solo_v1` 0.941 / `ffsolo_v1` 0.944) and load-bearing — it prices every open lever —
  so this checkpoint is shipped alone to measure it at n=2. The seat decline above stands unchanged.

labelwise_dense_drop40_long_ff
  The third refit, ABANDONED at epoch 2 and correctly so: `.partial` on disk, no `.pt`, no holdout record
  (a `--fold -1` arm holds nothing out). It was not re-armed once `fullseat2_v1` read the second dose flat,
  because the third dose of a lever measured flat at doses one and two is a ~0-expected run. Written down
  because an abandoned `.partial` beside two finished siblings LOOKS like unfinished work: on 2026-09-17 it
  was read as the champion's last untaken seat upgrade and priced as a free lever, and only the board rows
  said otherwise. A decision that lives in five entries' prose and nowhere on this file is a decision the
  next reader pays to make twice.

labelwise_dense_stem_s2026
  NOT A DECISION ABOUT THE STEM — a dead arm. Died at epoch 4 of the 24 its recipe asked for with
  `trainer exit 127`, the venv-rewrite signature, at 2026-09-16T21:04Z; `convergence_check` reads
  TRUNCATED, best e3 of 4 run. The `.holdout.npz` beside it is therefore an epoch-3 read of a
  24-epoch schedule and prices nothing — under `one_cycle` the anneal never ran, so any number taken
  off it is a floor, not the member. No `.pt` exists and none will. The log's last lines also record
  3705 MiB leaving the card mid-run (another process, by arrangement), so the run was already
  degraded before it died. The question it was armed to answer — whether an open stem carries at
  224 px — is LIVE in `labelwise_dense_stem_llrd_s2026`, which is the relaunch plus layer decay.
  Declined so the npz cannot be scored as a member; re-open nothing here, read that arm instead.

labelwise_dense_stem_llrd_s2026
  The open-stem question, answered and NEGATIVE. 24 of 24 epochs run (convergence_check silent on it),
  peak e19, priced against its own family sibling on the same 909 rows and the same budget:
  `labelwise_dense_drop40_long_s2026` LEDGER 0.8672 / LEDGER75 0.9048 against this arm's 0.8667 / 0.9007.
  So `--unfreeze-stem --layer-decay 0.75` at 224 px costs -0.0005 at midpoint and **-0.0041 at LEDGER75**,
  against a pre-registered 0.8712 it missed by 0.0045. Declined as a seat because it is strictly worse
  than the member already sitting in the champion, on the cut a seat is chosen by.
  It does NOT attribute the loss — stem and layer decay moved together in one arm, so neither is
  individually convicted. What it does settle is the 336 + open-stem cell, which was armable only if the
  224 result said the stem carried at all; it reads negative on both cuts, so that cell is closed unrun.
  Re-open only by separating the two flags, and price that against the -0.0041, not against zero.

erase_own_cells_s2026_run1
  The VOID first run of `erase_own_cells`, kept deliberately and declined deliberately. Its sweep npz was
  keyed on SEATS while `PatchErasing.loaded` joins by StudyInstanceUID, so nothing joined and every study
  erased at the MEAN map: it measures a blind generic cutout, not the treatment it is named for. Not
  shippable under that name and not worth shipping under any other -- LEDGER 0.8541 / LEDGER75 0.8899,
  which is -0.0015 on `probe224_control_s2026` at midpoint.
  It is KEPT because it is now the only measurement this project owns of a blind cutout at this rate and
  grid, and it is the control that makes the repaired run's +0.0021 / +0.0029 over it readable. Deleting
  it would cost a reading that can only be re-taken with a card.
  Declined as a member and as a seat. Never quote it as `erase_own_cells`.

erase_own_cells_s2026
  Map-driven targeted erasing, answered and FLAT on both cuts. Blanks one cell of each study's own
  4x4 occlusion map per pass at rate 0.25, the maps swept from `labelwise_dense_drop40_long_s2026` over
  3498 train studies. Read ALONE against its own control, 12 epochs, 909 rows, the comparison the arm
  pre-registered:
    probe224_control_s2026   LEDGER 0.8556 / LEDGER75 0.8914   e10
    erase_own_cells_s2026    LEDGER 0.8562 / LEDGER75 0.8928   e11
  +0.0006 at midpoint and +0.0014 at LEDGER75, against a 0.0062 holdout floor over these same 909 rows.
  Flat on both, and the two cuts agree in sign, which is what makes it a flat rather than a cut artefact
  (knee-f9aao).
  THE NUMBERS ABOVE REPLACE A VOID PAIR (-0.0015 / -0.0015), and the reason is the constraint here: the
  sweep npz recorded SEATS while `PatchErasing.loaded` joins by StudyInstanceUID, so the join matched
  NOTHING and every study fell to the mean-map fallback. That arm measured a generic cutout under this
  treatment's name. `patch_map_rekey` re-keys a sweep onto names verified against the truth it stored,
  and `loaded` now REFUSES a sweep naming none of the corpus rather than degrading to the mean. The
  repaired run logs `909 of 4407 studies are absent` -- exactly the holdout, so the 3498 swept studies
  joined and the axis is measured for the first time.
  The void run is kept as `erase_own_cells_s2026_run1` and is now a free second reading: generic mean-map
  cutout scores 0.8541 / 0.8899, i.e. -0.0015 on the control. So targeted beats generic by +0.0021 /
  +0.0029 and both sit inside the floor -- the map buys a little over a blind cutout, and a blind cutout
  costs a little, and neither clears.
  `member_axes` calls it DISTINCT (rank 0.9037, js 0.0092, maha 2.469) and that did not buy it a seat --
  its js sits AT the roster boundary, so distinct on one axis only. THE PRIOR seat_add IS VOID TOO: it
  was run over the void checkpoint. This member has no measured seat value; do not quote -0.0004.
  It does NOT convict the mechanism's premise. The premise is measured and stands: border-only reads
  0.8006 against centre-only 0.8021, so redundant evidence exists, and the maps are per-study (modal
  cell 26.8%, labels agree on a study's peak cell 19.2% of the time). What is priced is ONE rate at ONE
  grid for ONE budget -- 0.25 at 4x4 over 12 epochs -- and a null there says the augmentation as
  configured buys nothing, not that map-driven erasing cannot. Re-open only by moving grid or rate with
  an argument for the new value, and price it against this +0.0014, never against zero.
  Declined as a member; the npz stays so the comparison can be re-read.

solobase_hold
  NOT A MEMBER — a byte-identical copy of labelwise_depth24_drop40_long_s2026, the champion member.
  md5 6c6aab320deca08a94c1a7fe8cf4924a on the .pt and d27edf77e383d58a3b2cd46c0fea0c42 on the
  .holdout.npz, both matching, so every reading it can produce is the champion's own: ledger75 0.9053
  at epoch 18 over 909 rows, and tail overlap 1.000 against itself on tail_screen. It exists because the
  checkpoint was copied to a speakable name and the copy kept its holdout record.
  It may NEVER be seated beside its original — an ensemble that takes both is one member voting twice,
  and nothing downstream compares hashes. Delete the copy or keep it declined; do not roster it.

labelwise_dense_drop40_long_ff
  TRUNCATED at 3 of the 24 epochs its recipe asked for (convergence_check, knee-efv7c). Declined 2026-09-17
  rather than re-run, and the reason is arithmetic, not doubt about the member.
  It is a dark arm: fitted at --fold -1, so it holds nothing out and NO ledger row can ever price it.
  Its fold-0 twin labelwise_dense_drop40_long_s2026 is complete, reads 0.9048 ledger75 at epoch 23, and is
  already a seat in the pinned champion tripleseat_v2 — so the recipe is shipping, in its readable form.
  The only thing a 24-epoch re-run could buy is substituting this for that seat, on the theory that a
  full-corpus fit beats an 80% one. That is a SEAT swap in an unweighted 7-mean, so it moves the ensemble
  by delta/7, and the closed record brackets the dilution at 4.7x-9.8x on three separate levers.
  Against ~3-4 h of card for a member that cannot be read offline, that is the worst ratio on the shelf.
  Re-open only if a single-member entry is ever built on this recipe, where the /7 does not apply.

sam_r01_s2026
  Sharpness-aware minimisation at rho=0.01 on the calibrated dense recipe. ledger75 0.8981 at epoch 10,
  CONVERGED, against its own pre-registered control calib_dense_hold_s2026 at 0.8949 — a directional
  +0.0032, inside the 0.0062 paired floor and inside the band the arm declared before it ran. SAM costs
  two forward-backward passes per step, so the recipe is twice the card for a delta the ruler cannot
  resolve. Both numbers are ledger75 on population 66c6f231.
  Re-open only on a mechanism that predicts SAM should pay MORE at a larger rho or a longer schedule, and
  price it against the doubled step cost — not by re-running rho=0.01 and reading the same flat again.

recal_labelwise_s2026
  TRUNCATED. Best epoch 1 of 2 recorded, against 12 asked for by its own recipe. convergence_check reports
  it non-zero, and under one_cycle the anneal never ran, so its 0.8392 is a FLOOR and not a reading of
  anything. This is a dead process, not a member: it is declined for having no number, not for having a
  bad one.
  Re-open by RE-RUNNING it to its declared 12 epochs. Nothing about the recalibrated-label axis is
  measured either way until something does.

lr8e5_ship_s2026
  The shipping corner at backbone_lr 8e-5, twice the 4e-5 it has carried for 41 arms. ledger75 0.9031
  against its matched control labelwise_depth24_drop40_long_s2026 at 0.9053 — a directional -0.0022,
  inside the 0.0062 paired floor. CONVERGED 24/24, and both runs peak at the same epoch 18, so this is a
  flat rather than a schedule artefact. Both numbers are ledger75 on population 66c6f231.
  The arm's own mechanism is REFUTED rather than merely unpaid. It argued the corner was
  regularisation-bound and would take more rate: instead the doubled rate FITS LESS at every matched
  epoch (train loss 0.5973 at e22 against the control's 0.5600 at e23), which is what a higher peak rate
  adding gradient noise looks like, not what underfitting looks like.
  Re-open only on a mechanism that predicts a rate change should pay at 24 epochs under slot_dropout 0.40
  — the 12-epoch tilt that motivated this arm does not survive the doubled schedule, and re-running any
  point on that tilt reads this same flat.

vjepa_cold_s2026
  0.7499 midpoint / 0.7614 ledger75 @holdout909, peak epoch 9 of 12 — converged, not budget-bound.
  0.144 below the honest best solo (labelwise_depth24_drop40_long_s2026, 0.9053 ledger75). Nothing at
  this distance is shippable or seatable.
  THE DECLINE IS ABOUT THIS HARNESS, NOT ABOUT V-JEPA2. trajectory_check called it before the run
  finished (CANNOT REACH, generous ceiling 0.7753) and the pre-registered <0.82 branch names the
  suspect: this arm ran the ViT-L trunk at 336 px over a 20-slice stack, while the SAME trunk read
  0.8640 (vjepa_member) and 0.8673 (vjepa_warm) ledger75 at its NATIVE 256 px over 12 slices. A 0.10
  gap between two harnesses on one trunk is a fact about the harness.
  Re-open by running this trunk at 256 px / 12 slices under the current recipe and beating its own
  0.8673, not by re-quoting either number.

headlr_floor_s2026
  The shipping corner at head_lr 5e-5, the third point on an axis whose other two are 1e-3
  (labelwise_depth24_drop40_long_s2026, 0.9053) and 2e-4 (headlr_ship_s2026, 0.9059). ledger75 0.9011,
  the WORST of the three by 0.0048 against the top — directional, inside the 0.0062 paired floor, and
  converged at peak epoch 17 of 24. It was bought as a SHAPE rather than a candidate: two points cannot
  separate "monotone down" from "past a flat top", and the third says the axis turns over, 2e-4 is not
  an edge, and there is nothing below it. Declined as a member, kept as the reading that closed the axis
  (knee-h25fx). Re-open only by beating 0.9059 at the corner, not by re-quoting this number.

unfreeze_deep_s2026
  Seven adapting encoder blocks against the default six, band_dense_s2026's flag line byte for byte
  plus --unfreeze-last 7. Converged, peak epoch 9 of 12, 909 studies. It reads 0.8585 midpoint /
  0.8865 ledger75 against the control's 0.8578 / 0.8877: +0.0007 on one ruler and -0.0012 on the
  other, both far inside the 0.0062 paired floor, and the better ruler carries the negative sign.
  The arm existed because the segment already measured RISES — 4 blocks 0.8320, 6 blocks 0.8578,
  +0.0129 per block — and asked whether that gradient survives past six. It does not: the curve has
  peaked at or before six, and the flag is at its optimum where we ship. Declined as a member, kept
  as the reading that closed the adaptation-depth axis (knee-p724l). Re-open only by beating 0.8877
  ledger75 at that corner, never by re-quoting the 4->6 slope.

soup_anneal2_s2026
  A uniform weight average of `headlr_ship_s2026` and `labelwise_depth24_drop40_long_s2026`, built to
  test whether weight-space souping fails only at a high-LR anneal point. It does not: 0.8662 midpoint
  / 0.9016 ledger75, below BOTH parents and on the K-law's -0.0044 per doubling. Declined as a member,
  kept as the reading that closed the weight-soup axis on its second explanation. Re-open only with
  members sharing a fine-tuned basin, never with a larger K.

slicecover36_s2026
  36-slice coverage against the 24-slice shipping recipe. It DIED AT EPOCH 6 OF 24 (convergence_check:
  TRUNCATED, best e6 of 7 run, silent, mid-step 248/583, 19.93 GB peak against a 22 GB cap - not an OOM),
  so its 0.8557 ledger75 is a FLOOR and may never be quoted as the recipe's number. The axis is closed
  anyway, and on better evidence than a final score: the MATCHED-EPOCH trajectory over the seven epochs it
  lived, on the same 909-study holdout, against TWO independent 24-slice controls that agree with each
  other to within 0.003 (headlr_ship_s2026, labelwise_depth24_drop40_long_s2026).

    epoch    36 slices   headlr_ship   depth24_long   deficit
      0        0.7195      0.7238         0.7380      -0.0185
      1        0.7381      0.7557         0.7705      -0.0324
      2        0.7640      0.7851         0.7948      -0.0308
      3        0.7929      0.8114         0.8080      -0.0185
      4        0.8054      0.8252         0.8293      -0.0239
      5        0.8202      0.8310         0.8309      -0.0108
      6        0.8255      0.8447         0.8476      -0.0221

  Behind at every one of seven epochs, mean -0.0224, which is 3.6x the 0.0062 paired floor, and 50% slower
  per epoch (331 s against 222 s). Truncation moves a FINAL score; it cannot move a whole matched
  trajectory, so the early death is not the explanation for this and the deficit is the slice count. Re-open
  only by naming a mechanism that inverts a deficit which is stable-to-widening across the ramp - not by
  running the same recipe to 24 epochs, which prices at ~2.2 h for P(clears the floor) ~ 0.10.

dml_a_s2026
  DECLINED ON MERIT 2026-09-18 -- the arm completed all 24 epochs across three rounds and read 0.9044
  ledger75 (0.8687 midpoint, peak e18) against its control `headlr_ship_s2026` 0.9059 (0.8702, peak e17):
  -0.0015, which is knee-z81wd's fourth pre-registered branch and closes mutual supervision for this
  family at w=0.3. The peak is e18 and not the last epoch, so this is a verdict and not a void reading.
  The per-epoch deficit never crossed zero and never narrowed past -0.0008 (e16 -0.0024, e18 -0.0008,
  e23 -0.0009), so it is a flat loss rather than a late payer cut off by the budget.
  `convergence_check` called this run TRUNCATED until 2026-09-18; that was a false positive on every
  `--resume`d run and is FIXED in `7695751c` -- it now reads the last row's own epoch index instead of
  counting rows, so this run reads CONVERGED at 24 of 24 (knee-s0c4o, closed).
  Re-open by answering the flat curve -- a different weight, supervision at a different layer, or a
  teacher that is not also a student -- never by re-quoting this number.

dml_a_s2026_run1
  INTERMEDIATE BY CONSTRUCTION -- the round-1 checkpoint of the mutual-supervision schedule, written to a
  `_run1` name when round 2 took over the base name `dml_a_s2026`. It is the SAME weights the base name
  held at the end of round 1, so it can never be a distinct ensemble seat; shipping it would ship an
  earlier stopping point of a member already under decision (knee-z81wd). It is kept on disk only so the
  round-over-round trajectory can be read. Re-open only if round 2 is ever found to have REGRESSED against
  it by more than the 0.0062 paired floor, which would make round 1 the member and round 2 the discard.

dml_b_s2026_run1
  INTERMEDIATE BY CONSTRUCTION -- the same, for the peer arm. `dml_b` exists to supervise `dml_a`; the
  deliverable of knee-z81wd is a SINGLE solo checkpoint, so no `dml_b` checkpoint of any round is a
  shipping candidate. Re-open only if `dml_b` is ever re-registered as a member in its own right, which
  would need its own holdout reading against `headlr_ship_s2026` 0.9059 ledger75.

dml_a_s2026_run2
  INTERMEDIATE BY CONSTRUCTION -- the round-2 checkpoint of the mutual-supervision schedule, set aside
  under a `_run2` name exactly as `dml_a_s2026_run1` above. A fold-0 half whose line ships only as the
  whole-corpus refit `dml_b_ff`; the deliverable is a single solo checkpoint (knee-z81wd, knee-8bmi2).

dml_b_s2026_run2
  INTERMEDIATE BY CONSTRUCTION -- the same, for the peer arm. Its line ships as `dml_b_ff` (knee-8bmi2).
resnet_224_ext_s2026
  PARTIAL TRANSFER, BELOW THE VIT ON THE SAME RECIPE -- 0.8948 @ledger75 on the 909, converged (best
  e11 of 12 asked). It is +0.0106 over resnet_224_short 0.8842 and short of the >= 0.8990 the arm
  pre-registered, so the five-term recipe that lifted the ViT +0.025 to labelwise_ext_drop40_long
  0.9045 carries less than half of that onto a resnet50d trunk. Kept as the CNN-trunk reading of
  knee-k01q0, not a shipping candidate: the deliverable is one solo and the ViT solo is 0.0097 above it.
